    {
      "id": "1akTfwCLl595tawHsHmRb",
      "type": "text",
      "x": -24993.04561758367,
      "y": -792.9287045022274,
      "width": 1460.433349609375,
      "height": 260.7104046713659,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1e1e1e",
      "backgroundColor": "transparent",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 1,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "b6uY",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 876135170,
      "version": 6437,
      "versionNonce": 870977566,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": [
        {
          "id": "Y8L7iA0oH1o3xtx-Ys5Y3",
          "type": "arrow"
        },
        {
          "id": "zkiH4TAmfgkWiPG0w_vGO",
          "type": "arrow"
        }
      ],
      "updated": 1745454398919,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "Applicability\n\n- Use the Template Method pattern when you want to let clients extend only particular\nsteps of an algorithm, but not the whole algorithm or its structure.\n- Use the pattern when you have several classes that contain almost identical algorithms\nwith some minor differences. As a result, you might need to modify all classes when the\nalgorithm changes.",
      "fontSize": 29.79547481958467,
      "fontFamily": 8,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "Applicability\n\n- Use the Template Method pattern when you want to let clients extend only particular steps of an algorithm, but not the whole algorithm or its structure.\n- Use the pattern when you have several classes that contain almost identical algorithms with some minor differences. As a result, you might need to modify all classes when the algorithm changes.",
      "autoResize": false,
      "lineHeight": 1.25
    },
    {
      "id": "ZKepGceMqbjhouzFbTOzf",
      "type": "text",
      "x": -22208.04561758367,
      "y": 1553.6594302633976,
      "width": 1460.433349609375,
      "height": 260.7104046713659,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1e1e1e",
      "backgroundColor": "transparent",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 1,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "b6uYV",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 444854338,
      "version": 6527,
      "versionNonce": 444725022,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": [
        {
          "id": "Y8L7iA0oH1o3xtx-Ys5Y3",
          "type": "arrow"
        },
        {
          "id": "ogmrf-FYnWQl0Ua2Ju8s3",
          "type": "arrow"
        }
      ],
      "updated": 1745456812820,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "Applicability\n\n- Use the Visitor when you need to perform an operation on all elements of a complex\nobject structure (for example, an object tree).\n- Use the Visitor to clean up the business logic of auxiliary behaviors.\n- Use the pattern when a behavior makes sense only in some classes of a class hierarchy,\nbut not in others.",
      "fontSize": 29.79547481958467,
      "fontFamily": 8,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "Applicability\n\n- Use the Visitor when you need to perform an operation on all elements of a complex object structure (for example, an object tree).\n- Use the Visitor to clean up the business logic of auxiliary behaviors.\n- Use the pattern when a behavior makes sense only in some classes of a class hierarchy, but not in others.",
      "autoResize": false,
      "lineHeight": 1.25
    },
