    {
      "id": "MWPZoM7_6JGlaRDKP6lol",
      "type": "text",
      "x": 293.8906040855786,
      "y": -7186.536014323926,
      "width": 1338.7666015625,
      "height": 633.1538399161743,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1e1e1e",
      "backgroundColor": "transparent",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 1,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [
        "Dm3TeWWYEEyFULkqagy-z"
      ],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "b6v8",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 675673129,
      "version": 4145,
      "versionNonce": 1037469116,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": [],
      "updated": 1745108304322,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "Applicability\n\n- Lazy initialization (virtual proxy). This is when you have a heavyweight \nservice object that wastes system resources by being always up, even though \nyou only need it from time to time.\n- Access control (protection proxy). This is when you want only specific clients \nto be able to use the service object; for instance, when your objects are crucial\nparts of an operating system and clients are various launched applications.\n- Local execution of a remote service (remote proxy). This is when the service \nobject is located on a remote server.\n- Logging requests (logging proxy). This is when you want to keep a history of \nrequests to the service object.\n- Caching request results (caching proxy). This is when you need to cache results\nof client requests and manage the life cycle of this cache, especially if results\nare quite large.\n-  Smart reference. This is when you need to be able to dismiss a heavyweight \nobject once there are no clients that use it.",
      "fontSize": 29.79547481958467,
      "fontFamily": 8,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "Applicability\n\n- Lazy initialization (virtual proxy). This is when you have a heavyweight \nservice object that wastes system resources by being always up, even though \nyou only need it from time to time.\n- Access control (protection proxy). This is when you want only specific clients \nto be able to use the service object; for instance, when your objects are crucial \nparts of an operating system and clients are various launched applications.\n- Local execution of a remote service (remote proxy). This is when the service \nobject is located on a remote server.\n- Logging requests (logging proxy). This is when you want to keep a history of \nrequests to the service object.\n- Caching request results (caching proxy). This is when you need to cache results \nof client requests and manage the life cycle of this cache, especially if results \nare quite large.\n-  Smart reference. This is when you need to be able to dismiss a heavyweight \nobject once there are no clients that use it.",
      "autoResize": false,
      "lineHeight": 1.25
    },
