      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [
        "T_Q26Cc6grx5aHHSR60-g"
      ],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "b7T8",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 117401986,
      "version": 6234,
      "versionNonce": 77574722,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": [
        {
          "id": "Y8L7iA0oH1o3xtx-Ys5Y3",
          "type": "arrow"
        }
      ],
      "updated": 1745454065441,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "Applicability\n\n- Use the Strategy pattern when you want to use different variants of an algorithm within\nan object and be able to switch from one algorithm to another during runtime.\n- Use the Strategy when you have a lot of similar classes that only differ in the way\nthey execute some behavior.\n- Use the pattern to isolate the business logic of a class from the implementation\ndetails of algorithms that may not be as important in the context of that logic.\n- Use the pattern when your class has a massive conditional statement that switches\nbetween different variants of the same algorithm.",
      "fontSize": 29.79547481958467,
      "fontFamily": 8,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "Applicability\n\n- Use the Strategy pattern when you want to use different variants of an algorithm within an object and be able to switch from one algorithm to another during runtime.\n- Use the Strategy when you have a lot of similar classes that only differ in the way they execute some behavior.\n- Use the pattern to isolate the business logic of a class from the implementation details of algorithms that may not be as important in the context of that logic.\n- Use the pattern when your class has a massive conditional statement that switches between different variants of the same algorithm.",
      "autoResize": false,
      "lineHeight": 1.25
    },
